{"exports":["query"],"module":"api","notes":"Public surface: `query` is the ONLY public name in minidb/api.py; minidb/__init__.py (provided by the harness) re-exports `query` from here and `QueryError` from minidb/errors.py, so dependents must import QueryError from minidb.errors, not from api.\n\nInputs: `tables` is not copied or mutated; keys are table names (case-sensitive), values are lists of row dicts mapping column name to value. A table's column order is the key order of its first row; an empty table list therefore contributes no columns to `*` expansion. `sql` keywords are case-insensitive, identifiers case-sensitive. Passing anything other than a str for `sql` or a dict of lists of dicts for `tables` is reported as QueryError, not TypeError.\n\nOutputs: a new list of new dicts is returned; callers may mutate it freely. Values are the Python values taken from the input rows or computed by the engine (None represents SQL NULL; AVG yields float; division by zero yields None). An aggregate select list with no GROUP BY always yields exactly one row, even over zero input rows; otherwise zero result rows yield an empty list.\n\nError behaviour: api performs no validation of its own beyond the wrapping described above; all diagnostics originate in tokens/parser/planner/engine, and their QueryError messages are propagated unchanged (message text is not part of the contract). api catches BaseException subclasses only where they are ordinary Exception instances; KeyboardInterrupt and SystemExit are allowed to propagate.\n\nDependency direction: api depends on parser.parse, planner.plan, engine.execute and errors.QueryError exactly as those modules publish them, and no module imports api."}